%% How one outbound effect is claimed, performed and settled. This is the mechanism
%% behind edge cases 7, 8, 11 and 14, and it mirrors what wf-20 and wf-21 actually do.
%% Guards out of Observed are evaluated in the order written, top to bottom.
stateDiagram-v2
    direction TB

    [*] --> Deriving

    Deriving: Derive the key once, then persist it on the queue row
    note right of Deriving
      v1:{domain}:{entity}:{id}:{occurrence}
      Never an execution id, never a timestamp,
      never an attempt counter. Retries read the key
      back off the queue row rather than recomputing
      it, so drifting inputs cannot mint a new key.
    end note

    Deriving --> Claiming

    Claiming: One statement, leasing the work and reading any existing claim
    note left of Claiming
      INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING RETURNING,
      alongside a SELECT of the existing row. The
      insert says whether we won; the select is how
      the Observed guards below have state to read.
    end note

    Claiming --> Owned: the insert returned a row, we own this effect
    Claiming --> Observed: zero rows, an earlier attempt already opened it

    Owned --> Calling

    Calling: call the provider
    Calling --> Settle: 2xx, record success and the provider reference
    Calling --> Backoff: 429, 408 or 5xx
    Calling --> Terminal: any other 4xx, or missing credentials
    Calling --> Ambiguous: timeout, socket reset, or a crash before settling

    Observed --> Settle: recorded succeeded, reuse the stored response
    Observed --> Terminal: recorded failed_permanent
    Observed --> Deferring: still presumed in flight, another worker may be mid-call
    Observed --> Calling: last attempt was refused, or reconciliation proved absence
    Observed --> Ambiguous: last attempt left no answer and no verdict

    Deferring: requeue shortly, do not touch the provider
    Deferring --> Claiming

    Ambiguous: Did the effect land? This is case 7
    Ambiguous --> Reconciling
    Reconciling: ask the provider, looking the effect up by key
    Reconciling --> Settle: found it, adopt the provider reference
    Reconciling --> Backoff: not found, and the verdict is recorded on the claim

    Backoff: requeue at random(0, min(2s x 2^n, 15m))
    Backoff --> Claiming: attempts remain
    Backoff --> Terminal: attempts exhausted

    Settle: write claim state, provider reference and the audit row together
    Settle --> Succeeded

    Terminal --> DeadLetter
    DeadLetter: dead_letter row, keeping the original key
    DeadLetter --> Claiming: operator replay, which clears only failed claims

    Succeeded --> [*]

    note left of Succeeded
      Idempotent by construction: every later attempt
      lands in Observed and short-circuits there.
      Case 8 is proven from outside, by asserting the
      provider journal recorded exactly one delivery.
    end note
